## 4. Hardware and Performance Analysis

### 4.1 Memory Bus Overhead & Cache-Line Contention

| Operation | Atomic Read Ordering | Atomic Write Ordering | Cache Bus Traffic (`MESI`) | Interconnect Overhead |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `Get()` (Read Path) | `memory_order_acquire` | **None** | Cacheline remains in **Shared (S)** state across sockets | $0\times$ RFO broadcasts, zero bus lock cycles |
| `Put()` (Write Path) | `memory_order_acquire` | `memory_order_acq_rel` (CAS) | Target bucket moves to **Modified (M)** state | Localized to hashed bucket cacheline |
| `PruneOldVersions()` | `memory_order_acquire` | `memory_order_acq_rel` (CAS) | Unlinked node isolated from global traversal | Retired to thread-local Limbo list without cross-thread invalidation |
| `EpochGuard` Pin/Unpin | `memory_order_acquire` | `memory_order_release` | Local `ThreadControlBlock` line updated | Private thread cacheline; zero inter-thread false sharing |

### 4.2 `io_uring` Kernel-Bypass Group Commit Pipeline
* **Direct-I/O Alignment:** Buffers are aligned to $4096$-byte boundaries (`SECTOR_SIZE`), bypassing the Linux page cache and double buffering overheads.
* **Lock-Free Flat Combining:** Client threads append their serial log descriptors into the lock-free MPSC queue via a single atomic swap (`exchange`). The asynchronous worker aggregates entries into $4\text{KB}$-aligned Direct-I/O blocks, submitting batches via `io_uring_prep_writev`/`io_uring_prep_write` with completions harvested via lock-free CQE polling, achieving high throughput under high contention.

