       Level 1       |  Node A (Internal)                         |
                     |  Keys: [ 50 ]  |  High-Key: +inf           |
                     |  Children: [ Ptr(Leaf L) , Ptr(Leaf R) ]   |-----> (nullptr)
                     +--------------------------------------------+
                                   /                \
                    +-------------+                  +-------------+
                    |                                              |
                    v                                              v
+------------------------------------+       +------------------------------------+
|  Node L (Leaf)                     |       |  Node R (Leaf)                     |
|  Keys: [ 10, 20, 30 ]              | ----> |  Keys: [ 60, 70, 80 ]              | ----> (nullptr)
|  High-Key: 50                      | Sibling|  High-Key: +inf                   |
+------------------------------------+       +------------------------------------+
```

#### 1.1 Lehman-Yao Invariants Adapted for Lock-Free State Transitions

A classic Lehman-Yao $B^{\text{link}}$-tree prevents read locks by introducing a **High-Key** and a **Right-Sibling Pointer** in every node (both internal and leaf). In our lock-free adaptation:

1. **Monotonic Key Range Invariant:**
   For any node $u$, let $K(u)$ be the set of keys stored in $u$, $\text{low\_key}(u)$ be the lower bound (implicit from parent or left sibling), and $\text{high\_key}(u)$ be the upper bound.
   $$\forall k \in K(u), \quad \text{low\_key}(u) \le k \le \text{high\_key}(u)$$
2. **Right-Link Invariant:**
   If a key $k > u.\text{high\_key}$, and $u.\text{right\_sibling} \neq \text{nullptr}$, the key $k$ is guaranteed to reside in $u.\text{right\_sibling}$ or a downstream right sibling along the same level. A concurrent reader detecting $k > u.\text{high\_key}$ simply traverses `right_sibling` via `memory_order_acquire` without backtracking or consulting the parent.
3. **Split Publication Invariant:**
   When node $L$ splits into $L$ and $R$:
   - $R$ is fully constructed in private memory: $R.\text{keys} \leftarrow L.\text{keys}[\text{mid}\dots N]$, $R.\text{right\_sibling} \leftarrow L.\text{right\_sibling}$, $R.\text{high\_key} \leftarrow L.\text{high\_key}$.
   - $R$ is published to the WAL before being linked into the in-memory tree.
   - $L.\text{high\_key}$ is set to $L.\text{keys}[\text{mid}-1]$ using `memory_order_release`.
   - $L.\text{right\_sibling}$ is atomically updated to $R$ using `memory_order_release`.
   - Any reader concurrent with the split observing $L.\text{right\_sibling} = R$ via `memory_order_acquire` is guaranteed to observe the fully initialized state of $R$.
4. **Epoch-Based Reclamation Safety Invariant:**
   A node $u$ retired at epoch $e$ can only be freed when all active threads have announced their presence in epoch $e' \ge e + 2$. Since thread epoch guards prevent reclamation of nodes reachable at the moment of guard acquisition, no reader can experience a Use-After-Free (UAF) or ABA pointer hazard.

---

#### 1.2 Memory Ordering Analysis

| Operation | Atomic Variable | Memory Order | Justification |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Sibling Read** | `node->right_sibling` | `acquire` | Synchronizes with the split release write; ensures node contents of the right sibling are fully visible. |
| **Sibling Link** | `left->right_sibling.store(right)` | `release` | Publishes the newly initialized right node and its contents to concurrent readers. |
| **High-Key Read** | `node->high_key` | `acquire` | Ensures correct ordering when checking if traversal must shift right. |
| **EBR Reader Enter** | `thread_state->epoch` | `release` / `relaxed` | Publishes thread active status to the global epoch collector without full bus locks. |
| **EBR Reader Exit** | `thread_state->active` | `release` | Guarantees all read-side dereferences complete before epoch exit is visible. |
| **WAL Sequence Alloc** | `global_lsn` | `relaxed` / `fetch_add` | Guarantees unique monotonic LSN assignment; ordering enforced by dependent batch fences. |

