### 1. Architecture Design & Formal Invariant Proofs

```
                      +-------------------+
                      |   Root Pointer    | (atomic<Node*>)
                      +---------+---------+
                                |
                                v
               Level 1   +-------------+  right   +-------------+
                         | Inner NodeA +--------->+ Inner NodeB | -> nullptr
                         +------+------+ (acquire)|+------+------+
                                |                         |
            +-------------------+-------------------+     |
            | (acquire)                             |     |
            v                                       v     v
Level 0  +------+  right   +------+  right   +------+  right   +------+
(Leaves) | Leaf0+--------->+ Leaf1+--------->+ Leaf2+--------->+ Leaf3| -> nullptr
         +------+          +------+          +------+          +------+
        [k0..k10]         [k11..k20]        [k21..k30]        [k31..+inf]
        HighKey=10        HighKey=20        HighKey=30        HighKey=+inf
```

#### Lehman-Yao Lock-Free Invariants

1. **High-Key Bounding Invariant:**
   Every node $u$ maintains a boundary key $K_{\text{high}}(u)$. For every key $k$ located in the subtree rooted at $u$, $k \le K_{\text{high}}(u)$. If $u$ is the rightmost node at its level, $K_{\text{high}}(u) = +\infty$.

2. **Right-Sibling Link Invariant:**
   If a node $u$ splits into $u$ and $u'$, $u'$ is assigned $K_{\text{high}}(u') = K_{\text{high}}(u)$, and $u$ is updated such that $K_{\text{high}}(u) = K_{\text{split}}$ and $u \xrightarrow{\text{right}} u'$. A reader observing $k > K_{\text{high}}(u)$ traverses $u \to \text{right}$ via `memory_order_acquire` to reach $u'$ without acquiring locks or restarting from the root.

3. **Lock-Free Split & Publication Protocol:**
   - **Step 1 (Alloc & Initialize Right Sibling $u'$):** Populate $u'$ with the upper half of $u$'s keys and point $u' \to \text{right}$ to $u \to \text{right}$.
   - **Step 2 (Atomic Link):** Atomically update $u \to \text{right} = u'$ with `std::memory_order_release`. Concurrently or immediately preceding, set $K_{\text{high}}(u) = K_{\text{split}}$.
   - **Step 3 (Parent Update):** Insert the pair $(K_{\text{split}}, u')$ into $u$'s parent. If the parent is full, it splits recursively using the exact same protocol. If a concurrent search reaches $u$ before the parent pointer is installed, the search follows the sibling link $u \to u'$ horizontally.

#### Memory Ordering Correctness Analysis

- **Node Publication:** Writing keys and values into a newly allocated sibling $u'$ strictly precedes the atomic store updating $u \to \text{right} = u'$ via `std::memory_order_release`. Readers load `u->right` with `std::memory_order_acquire`, establishing a *synchronizes-with* relationship that guarantees all keys in $u'$ are visible.
- **Epoch Retirement:** Memory reclamation never frees a node $u$ until all active threads have advanced their thread-local epoch past the retirement epoch $e_{\text{retire}}$, ensuring zero dangling pointer dereferences.
- **WAL Durability Fence:** An atomic log sequence number (LSN) counter is updated via `std::memory_order_acq_rel`. Completion of the underlying I/O flush signals waiting client threads via release semantics on an atomic commit LSN.

