### 1. Mathematical & Formal Specification

#### 1.1 System Model & Definitions
Let $(\mathcal{P}, \le)$ be a totally ordered set of $P$ discrete priority levels $\{0, 1, \dots, P-1\}$, where priority $0$ denotes the highest priority. The data structure maintains a set of elements partitioned into $P$ priority bands. Each band $p \in \mathcal{P}$ consists of $K$ disjoint lock-free concurrent lanes $\mathcal{L}_{p} = \{\ell_{p, 0}, \ell_{p, 1}, \dots, \ell_{p, K-1}\}$.

Each lane $\ell_{p, k}$ is modeled as an atomic sequence of indexed turn-slots:
$$\ell_{p, k} = \langle s_0, s_1, \dots, s_{N-1} \rangle, \quad s_i = (\tau_i, v_i)$$
where $\tau_i \in \mathbb{N}$ is a monotonically advancing turn sequence counter, and $v_i \in \mathcal{T} \cup \{\bot\}$ is the element payload.

```
       Priority 0 (Highest)   Priority 1            ...   Priority P-1 (Lowest)
       +--------------------+ +--------------------+       +--------------------+
Lane 0 | Slot 0 | Slot 1 |..| | Slot 0 | Slot 1 |..|       | Slot 0 | Slot 1 |..|
Lane 1 | Slot 0 | Slot 1 |..| | Slot 0 | Slot 1 |..|       | Slot 0 | Slot 1 |..|
 ...   |        ...         | |        ...         |       |        ...         |
Lane K | Slot 0 | Slot 1 |..| | Slot 0 | Slot 1 |..|       | Slot 0 | Slot 1 |..|
       +--------------------+ +--------------------+       +--------------------+
          ▲                                                   ▲
          └────── Monotonic Hierarchical Priority Bitmask ────┘
```

#### 1.2 Linearization Points (LP)

1. **`try_push(Priority p, T val)`**:
   - **LP:** The successful atomic Compare-And-Swap (CAS) or Fetch-Add on the tail ticket of lane $\ell_{p, k}$ that reserves slot index $i = \text{ticket} \pmod N$, paired with the subsequent release store on turn sequence $\tau_i$:
     $$\text{LP}_{\text{push}}: \langle \tau_i.\text{store}(\text{ticket} + 1, \texttt{memory\_order\_release}) \rangle$$
   - **Bitmask Publication:** If lane $\ell_{p, k}$ was previously empty, an auxiliary linearization point occurs at the atomic Fetch-Or on the hierarchical priority bitmask:
     $$\text{LP}_{\text{active}}: \langle \mathcal{B}.\text{fetch\_or}(2^{p}, \texttt{memory\_order\_release}) \rangle$$

2. **`try_pop()`**:
   - **LP:** The successful atomic read-modify-write on turn sequence $\tau_i$ matching ticket $j$ in slot $i$ of the selected highest-priority lane $\ell_{p, k}$:
     $$\text{LP}_{\text{pop}}: \langle \tau_i.\text{compare\_exchange\_strong}(j + 1, j + N, \texttt{memory\_order\_acq\_rel}) \rangle$$
   - **Empty Queue Detection:** When the root summary mask evaluates to $0$ under $\texttt{memory\_order\_acquire}$ twice consecutively without an intervening push event.

#### 1.3 Bound Staleness Guarantees ($\epsilon$-Staleness)
Let $t \in \mathbb{R}^+$ be the physical wall-clock time. If an element $x$ with priority $p_x$ is enqueued at $t_1$, and a subsequent `try_pop()` at $t_2 > t_1$ returns an element $y$ with priority $p_y$ such that $p_y > p_x$ (a priority inversion):
$$\Delta \tau = |p_y - p_x| \le \epsilon$$
Under the power-of-two randomized work-stealing strategy over $K$ lanes within priority window $[p_{\min}, p_{\min} + \epsilon]$, the probability of experiencing a staleness violation exceeding $\epsilon$ bands decays exponentially:
$$\mathbb{P}(\text{Staleness} > \epsilon) \le \left(1 - \frac{1}{K}\right)^{2 \cdot \epsilon \cdot K} \xrightarrow{K \ge 4} \mathcal{O}(e^{-2\epsilon})$$

#### 1.4 Memory Safety & Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR) Invariant
Let $E_g \in \mathbb{N}$ be the monotonically advancing global epoch. Each active thread $T_i$ publishes its observed epoch $e_i \in \{E_g, E_g - 1, \text{INACTIVE}\}$.
- **Safety Invariant:** A retired object $O$ retired at epoch $E_{\text{retire}} = e$ is guaranteed to have no active thread referencing it once:
  $$\forall T_i \in \text{ThreadsActive}, \quad e_i \ge e + 2 \quad \lor \quad e_i = \text{INACTIVE}$$
- **ABA Freedom:** All cyclic buffer indices are parameterized with monotonic generations derived from $\lfloor \text{ticket} / N \rfloor$, ensuring distinct turns across slot lifecycles.

