## 3. Algorithmic Complexity & NUMA Benchmark Analysis

### 3.1 Complexity Profile

| Operation | Time Complexity (Average) | Time Complexity (Worst-Case Contention) | Memory Overhead per Op | Progress Guarantee |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Point Read (`Get`)** | $\mathcal{O}(\log N + L_v)$ | $\mathcal{O}(N + L_v)$ | $0$ allocations | Lock-Free / Wait-Free traversal |
| **Snapshot Scan (`RangeScan`)** | $\mathcal{O}(\log N + K \cdot L_v)$ | $\mathcal{O}(N + K \cdot L_v)$ | $\mathcal{O}(1)$ local array | Lock-Free |
| **Point Insert (`Insert`)** | $\mathcal{O}(\log N + \overline{H})$ | $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot H)$ | $1\ \text{Node} + 1\ \text{Version}$ | Lock-Free |
| **Logical Delete (`Delete`)** | $\mathcal{O}(\log N)$ | $\mathcal{O}(N)$ | $1\ \text{Version}$ | Lock-Free |
| **Version GC (`TriggerGC`)** | $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot L_v)$ | $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot L_v)$ | Amortized $\mathcal{O}(1)$ free | Non-blocking / Cooperative |

*(Where $N = \text{number of active keys}$, $H = 32$, $\overline{H} = \frac{1}{1-p} \approx 2$, $L_v = \text{active version chain length}$, $K = \text{scan limit}$)*

---

### 3.2 Memory Overhead Derivations

1. **Per-Node Memory Footprint**:
   * Skip-List Node Structure:
     $$\text{Size}(\text{Node}) = \text{sizeof}(\text{Key}) + \text{sizeof}(\text{size\_t}) + 8\ \text{bytes} (\text{version\_head}) + 32 \times 8\ \text{bytes} (\text{next towers}) \approx 280\ \text{bytes}$$
     With cache-line alignment (`alignas(64)`), each node consumes $\lceil 280 / 64 \rceil \times 64 = 320\ \text{bytes}$.
   * MVCC Version Node:
     $$\text{Size}(\text{VersionNode}) = 8\ (\text{tx\_begin}) + 8\ (\text{tx\_end}) + 1\ (\text{tombstone}) + \text{sizeof}(\text{Value}) + 8\ (\text{next}) \approx 32 + \text{sizeof}(\text{Value})\ \text{bytes}$$
     Aligned to cache line $\to 64\ \text{bytes}$.

2. **HEHR Memory Amortization**:
   * Thread Control Block: $64\ \text{bytes} \times \text{MaxThreads}$ (e.g., $64 \times 64 = 4\ \text{KB}$ total).
   * Retired Node Queue: $R \times 24\ \text{bytes} = 1024 \times 24 = 24.5\ \text{KB}$ per active writer thread. Memory reclamation occurs strictly in batches of $R = 1024$, amortizing epoch scan overhead down to $\mathcal{O}(\frac{\text{MaxThreads}}{R}) \approx \mathcal{O}(0.06)$ cycles per deallocation.

---

### 3.3 NUMA-Scale Contention Bottlenecks & Mitigations

```
Cross-NUMA Interconnect (QPI / UPI / Ultra-Path)
[Socket 0: Cores 0-31] <=====================> [Socket 1: Cores 32-63]
  |                                              |
  +-- L3 Cache (Node A: Level 31 next)            +-- L3 Cache (Node A: Level 31 next)
        * Invalidation Storms during Splicing           * Cache-Line Bouncing on Pred->Next
```

1. **Shared Head/Tail Contention**:
   * *Problem*: Head pointer array links at levels $H-1 \dots 16$ experience concurrent CAS updates from all threads across sockets, causing cross-socket cache-line invalidation storms via UPI/Infinity Fabric.
   * *Mitigation*: The implementation uses probabilistic height capping ($\mathbb{E}[H] = 2$). Higher tower levels are updated only for $p^{16} = 1.5 \times 10^{-5}$ of insertions.
2. **False Sharing Elimination**:
   * Every `ThreadControlBlock` and `Node` is aligned to `hardware_destructive_interference_size` (64/128 bytes), preventing false sharing between thread epoch slots and adjacent nodes during CAS loops.
3. **Hazard Escalation Synchronization Fence**:
   * The `RangeScan` hazard registration leverages an explicit `std::atomic_thread_fence(std::memory_order_seq_cst)` only when setting a hazard pointer, preventing costly full-system bus locks on the fast-path `Get` traversals. Fast-path reads rely exclusively on single-cycle `acquire` loads.

