### 3. Hardware-Level Optimization Analysis

#### Cache-Line & Spatial Locality (`alignas(64)`)
- **False Sharing Elimination:** Every instance of `ThreadEpochState`, `NodeHeader`, and WAL ring buffer atomic cursors (`head_`, `tail_`, `flushed_lsn_`) is aligned to distinct 64-byte boundaries via `alignas(CACHE_LINE_SIZE)`. This prevents invalidation storms across L1/L2 caches when threads concurrently write to independent cursors on the same bus.
- **Node Alignment:** Radix nodes are laid out so that hot traversal components (`version_lock`, `prefix_len`, `prefix[]`, and keys) are packed into the first 64-byte chunk. Lookup hits read only a single cache line per level.

#### Branchless SIMD Vectorization
- **Node16 Search:** Uses AVX2 / SSE4.2 intrinsics (`_mm_cmpeq_epi8` and `_mm_movemask_epi8`). The target byte is broadcast to a 128-bit register (`_mm_set1_epi8(byte)`), compared against 16 key bytes in a single instruction cycle, and the child offset is derived using branchless bit manipulation (`__builtin_ctz`):
  ```cpp
  __m128i cmp = _mm_cmpeq_epi8(_mm_set1_epi8(byte), _mm_loadu_si128((__m128i*)keys));
  int mask = _mm_movemask_epi8(cmp) & ((1 << count) - 1);
  if (mask) int idx = __builtin_ctz(mask);
  ```
- **Node48 Direct Indirection:** Keys map directly via a 256-byte dense index array where index lookups are constant-time $O(1)$ operations with zero branching (`idx = child_indices[byte]`).

#### Memory Ordering & Microarchitectural Memory Barriers

| Atomic Variable / Context | Memory Order | Architectural Justification |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `EpochManager::EnterCritical` | `seq_cst` fence | Enforces store-load order across heterogeneous CPU pipelines; prevents speculative loads of protected index pointers from being reordered *before* the thread's active state is globally visible to the epoch manager. |
| Version Chain Linking (`new_vnode->next`) | `release` / `acquire` | The writing thread uses `release` when linking a new version to ensure all payload bytes are globally coherent. Readers perform an `acquire` load, preventing out-of-order execution from reading payload data prior to acquiring the pointer. |
| Node Child CAS (`ReplaceChild`, `SetChild`) | `acq_rel` | Guarantees that node structural modifications (and prefix-length updates) are flushed to all CPU cores before the child pointer becomes reachable, while reading the latest valid state of previous pointers. |
| WAL Ring Cursors (`head_`, `flushed_lsn_`) | `acq_rel` / `acquire` | `fetch_add` on `head_` uses `acq_rel` to establish a synchronized reservation barrier between concurrent producers claiming overlapping segments. `flushed_lsn_` uses `release` on leader write-complete and `acquire` on follower futex wake. |

