### Formal Memory Ordering & Synchronization Proof

#### 1. Leader-Follower Group Commit Handoff
Let committer thread $T_i$ reserve slot index $S_i$ via:
$$S_i = \text{tail\_lsn.fetch\_add}(\text{frame\_size}, \text{std::memory\_order\_acq\_rel})$$

- **Payload Publishing**: $T_i$ formats and writes its binary frame into the DMA buffer at offset $S_i$. $T_i$ executes a release store on its slot status:
  $$\text{slot\_ready}[S_i].\text{store}(\text{READY}, \text{std::memory\_order\_release})$$
- **Leader Ingestion**: The elected leader $T_L$ monitors $\text{slot\_ready}[S_k]$. It reads with `std::memory_order_acquire`.
- **Proof against Data Races**: The store-release in $T_i$ synchronizes-with the load-acquire in $T_L$. Thus, all memory writes to the DMA payload at buffer offset $S_i$ happen-before $T_L$ dispatches the DMA segment to the kernel submission queue via `io_uring_enter`.
- **Completion Propagation**: Upon CQE receipt, $T_L$ publishes the newly persisted LSN:
  $$\text{persisted\_lsn.store}(L_{\text{max}}, \text{std::memory\_order\_release})$$
  Followers suspended on `persisted_lsn.wait(expected, std::memory_order_acquire)` observe this store. The release-acquire pair guarantees that all side effects up to $L_{\text{max}}$ are visible to followers before they return control to the caller.

#### 2. Lock-Free MVCC Version Chain Traversal
- **Writer Pointer Swing**: A writer prepends a new `VersionNode` $V_{\text{new}}$ to key $K$'s chain:
  $$V_{\text{new}}\to\text{next}.\text{store}(V_{\text{head}}, \text{std::memory\_order\_relaxed})$$
  $$K\to\text{head}.\text{store}(V_{\text{new}}, \text{std::memory\_order\_release})$$
- **Reader Traversal**: A concurrent reader executes:
  $$V_{\text{curr}} = K\to\text{head}.\text{load}(\text{std::memory\_order\_acquire})$$
  $$V_{\text{next}} = V_{\text{curr}}\to\text{next}.\text{load}(\text{std::memory\_order\_acquire})$$
- **Proof of Wait-Free Safety**: The store-release on $K\to\text{head}$ ensures that node initialization (commit LSN, payload data, pointer link) is fully committed to memory before $V_{\text{new}}$ becomes reachable. Readers traverse the linked list without modifying shared state (pure reads), guaranteeing $O(V)$ wait-free execution.

#### 3. Epoch-Based Memory Safety (EBR)
- Thread $T_R$ registers its active read epoch:
  $$\text{local\_epoch}.\text{store}(\text{global\_epoch}.\text{load}(\text{std::memory\_order\_relaxed}), \text{std::memory\_order\_seq\_cst})$$
  $$\text{active}.\text{store}(\text{true}, \text{std::memory\_order\_seq\_cst})$$
- A node $V_{\text{old}}$ is unlinked at epoch $E_{\text{unlink}}$ and placed into Limbo Bag $B[E_{\text{unlink}}]$.
- $B[E_{\text{unlink}}]$ is reclaimed only when:
  $$\forall T \in \text{RegisteredThreads}: T.\text{active} == \text{false} \lor T.\text{local\_epoch} > E_{\text{unlink}}$$
- **Proof of No Use-After-Free**: Because an active reader in epoch $E \le E_{\text{unlink}}$ pinned the epoch before or concurrently with the unlink operation, and the memory reclamation step observes all thread epoch states with acquire semantics, no node unlinked at $E_{\text{unlink}}$ will be freed while any thread is still accessing or can reach that node.

